REGIONAL REGULATION OF CENTRAL BANGKA REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2014

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF CENTRAL BANGKA,

Considering:

a. that women and men have equal rights to participate in various fields of life;
b. that women are national assets who play a very important role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations, therefore they need to receive protection from acts of violence and discrimination and need to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Empowerment and Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1);
3. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Elderly Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to State Gazette Number 3886);
5. Law Number 27 of 2000 concerning Establishment of Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 217, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 403);
6. Law Number 5 of 2003 concerning Establishment of South Bangka Regency, Central Bangka Regency, West Bangka Regency and East Belitung Regency in Bangka Belitung Islands Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 25, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4268);
7. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to State Gazette Number 4279);
8. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
9. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58);
11. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12);
12. Law Number 19 of 2011 concerning Ratification of Convention on The Rights of Persons With Disabilities (Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 107, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5251);
13. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2005 Number 165, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4593);
14. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Protection of Women;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF CENTRAL BANGKA REGENCY

and

REGENT OF CENTRAL BANGKA

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

Article 3

The implementation of Empowerment and Protection of Women is carried out with the aim of:
a. increasing the participation of women both individually and in groups as potential and resources in the implementation of social community life;
b. preventing all forms of discrimination;
c. empowering women both individually and in groups and communities experiencing gender issues so that they are able to meet their needs independently;
d. preventing and handling risks from women's vulnerability so that their survival can be fulfilled in accordance with minimum basic needs;
e. reducing the gap in opportunities between women and men in achieving development; and
f. reducing acts of violence against women.

CHAPTER III

HUMAN RIGHTS AND FUNDAMENTAL HUMAN FREEDOMS

Article 4

Human Rights and fundamental human freedoms, consist of:
a. Right to life;
b. Right to form a family and procreate;
c. Right to self-development;
d. Right to obtain justice;
e. Right to personal freedom;
f. Right to security;
g. Right to welfare; and
h. Right to participate in government.

Article 5

1. Every woman has the right to:
a. live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously physically and spiritually;
b. live in a family within the bonds of a lawful marriage;
c. maintain life and improve her standard of living;
d. have work that she likes and is also entitled to fair employment conditions;
e. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education in accordance with the specified requirements;
f. a healthy and clean environment;
g. receive fair wages in accordance with her work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity;
h. elect and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, village head elections and/or other political office elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to the applicable laws and regulations;
i. obtain special protection in the implementation of her work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health related to women's reproductive function; and
j. special rights inherent to women due to their reproductive function are guaranteed and protected by law.
2. Women who are adults and or are married have the right to perform legal acts independently unless otherwise determined by their religious law.
3. A lawful marriage can only take place based on the free will of the woman and the man in accordance with laws and regulations.
4. A wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband regarding all matters relating to their married life, relationship with their children and the right to ownership and management of joint property.
5. After the dissolution of her marriage, a woman has the same rights and responsibilities as her former husband regarding all matters relating to their children, taking into account the best interests of the child.
6. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has the same rights as her former husband regarding all matters relating to joint property without reducing the rights of the child, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
7. Every woman who is in a condition of disability, elderly, pregnant is entitled to receive facilities and special treatment.
8. Every elderly woman, with physical and/or mental disability, is entitled to receive care, education, training and special assistance at the expense of the regional government to ensure a decent life in accordance with human dignity.

Article 12

Implementation of policies, programs and activities for the protection of women, can be carried out with efforts to:
a. cooperate with the business world, non-governmental institutions, private companies, Foundations, and other domestic institutions as well as international institutions, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
b. conduct regional cooperation, in accordance with the provisions of the applicable laws and regulations;
c. provide and facilitate women's protection services;
d. compile a data system for women's protection; and
e. carry out coordination of the implementation of policies, programs and activities for the protection of women among Regional Work Units.

Article 13

1. Provision and facilitation of women's protection services as referred to in Article 12 letter c in the form of:
a. victim identification;
b. legal assistance;
c. medical rehabilitation;
d. psychosocial rehabilitation;
e. social reintegration;
f. repatriation assistance; org. other forms.
2. Provision and facilitation of women's protection services as referred to in paragraph (1) are provided to women:
a. victims of violence;
b. victims of human trafficking;
c. elderly who are not independent;
d. with disabilities;
e. victims of sexual exploitation;
f. heads of households;
g. in conflict-prone and disaster areas;
h. workers; and
i. other vulnerable groups.
